raw_response:
The schema contains these distinct entity types: vessel, AIS observation, observation location, absolute point geometry, velocity, orientation, three alternate vessel identifiers, ports in current/last/next roles, and geopolitical affiliations for vessel and port countries.

The vessel is identified by ship_id rather than MMSI or IMO. The name and description say ship_id is stable and provider-specific, while MMSI changes after re-flagging and IMO may be empty. I map the vessel to bsm:Vessel with 0.99 confidence based on the ship-prefixed fields and maritime values.

Each AIS report is a separate bsm:Observation identified by ship_id plus timestamp. This prevents reports for the same vessel from merging. Its associated location, point, velocity, and orientation use the same vessel-and-time key with distinct prefixes.

Latitude and longitude establish an bsm:AbsolutePoint. Because bsm:hasGeographicLocation leads to bsm:Location and bsm:Location connects to geometry through bsm:hasGeometry, the point is not folded directly into the observation.

Course maps to bsm:absoluteBearingAngle because the course description explicitly says it is the direction actually travelled from true north. Heading maps separately to bsm:headingAngle because it is the vessel bow direction. These near-synonyms are distinguished by the travelled-direction versus bow-direction qualifier.

The raw speed cannot map to bsm:speedMetersPerSecond because it is stored in tenths of a knot and the allowed transformations cannot perform division and unit conversion. Average and maximum voyage speeds are already in knots, not metres per second. Although max_speed resembles bsm:vesselMaximumSpeed, the “current voyage maximum recorded speed” qualifier does not establish the vessel’s inherent maximum speed, so it remains unmapped.

MMSI, IMO, and call sign are modeled as separate bsm:AlternateIdentifier entities because the schema provides several identifiers under distinct named standards. Their subjects use the respective identifier values, and each carries a constant standard code plus its value.

Current, last, and next ports are separate bsm:Port mappings because each role has a port name, UN/LOCODE, provider identifier, and country. UN/LOCODE is used for port identity because it is the cross-provider location code. The ontology provides no vessel-to-port or observation-to-port object property, so the port entities cannot be connected to the vessel without inventing a relation.

Flag and port-country codes populate bsm:GeopoliticalAffiliation entities through bsm:hasAffiliation. The full ship_country name has no property on that class; only bsm:geopoliticalAffiliationCode is available.

The voyage-related destination, ETAs, port departure time, distances, and voyage speed aggregates are not mapped. Modeling them as bsm:Action would lack a reliable voyage identifier and would risk merging distinct voyages.

All IRI transformations were checked against their referenced subjects. The observation, location, point, velocity, orientation, alternate-identifier, and affiliation reference expressions are character-for-character identical to their target subject transformations. No unused transformation keys are included.

